1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,388
This approval came 4 seconds late.

2
00:00:02,388 --> 00:00:03,736
So nothing was sent.

3
00:00:04,520 --> 00:00:04,965
Rewind.

4
00:00:04,965 --> 00:00:09,628
A small business holds dollars, and owes suppliers in euros and pounds.

5
00:00:09,828 --> 00:00:12,078
Rates keep moving while an approval waits.

6
00:00:13,228 --> 00:00:17,490
This is Rate-Locked Approval, on Airwallex's sandbox, with test money.

7
00:00:17,490 --> 00:00:19,832
A Claude model reads three invoices.

8
00:00:20,239 --> 00:00:24,203
Two come through clean. The third has a due time with no time zone,

9
00:00:24,203 --> 00:00:26,601
so the agent stops, and asks a person.

10
00:00:27,056 --> 00:00:30,277
Separately, five hostile invoices were thrown at it.

11
00:00:30,277 --> 00:00:32,781
Zero wrong values. All five were held.

12
00:00:36,032 --> 00:00:36,861
Next, the plan.

13
00:00:36,861 --> 00:00:38,946
Code does this part, not the model.

14
00:00:39,393 --> 00:00:41,339
The plan funds Acme from euros.

15
00:00:41,339 --> 00:00:44,228
Globex needs pounds, so dollars get converted.

16
00:00:44,228 --> 00:00:46,952
Initech waits for money that's coming in.

17
00:00:48,752 --> 00:00:51,846
Now the money Initech is waiting for looks less certain.

18
00:00:51,846 --> 00:00:54,869
Only the decisions that depend on it are recomputed.

19
00:00:57,049 --> 00:00:58,314
Acme is not touched.

20
00:00:58,314 --> 00:01:01,099
Globex is checked again, and stays the same.

21
00:01:01,099 --> 00:01:03,084
Initech now goes to a person.

22
00:01:05,734 --> 00:01:06,931
Here's the key idea.

23
00:01:06,931 --> 00:01:10,372
The agent may only spend at the rate a person approved.

24
00:01:11,929 --> 00:01:16,061
For Globex, it asks Airwallex for a quote: a rate with an end time.

25
00:01:16,061 --> 00:01:19,603
That opens a separate approver page, sized for a phone.

26
00:01:19,619 --> 00:01:23,702
They see what they pay, what the supplier gets, the locked rate, and a countdown.

27
00:01:23,828 --> 00:01:28,310
Approve in time, and the agent may use exactly this quote. Nothing else.

28
00:01:28,312 --> 00:01:31,656
The approver gets pulled into a meeting, and the clock runs out.

29
00:01:38,492 --> 00:01:41,668
The tap on Approve comes too late. The app refuses.

30
00:01:41,668 --> 00:01:46,551
Nothing was converted, nothing was paid. The log shows zero execution calls.

31
00:01:47,823 --> 00:01:51,344
So the agent asks for a fresh quote, and shows what changed.

32
00:01:51,344 --> 00:01:52,987
Eleven cents less this time.

33
00:01:52,987 --> 00:01:56,834
Either way, it won't convert without a quote a person approved.

34
00:01:57,956 --> 00:02:01,006
The approver says yes to the new quote, and it goes through.

35
00:02:08,699 --> 00:02:12,899
Now the record. Approved on the left, executed on the right, row by row.

36
00:02:12,899 --> 00:02:14,390
Twelve of twelve match.

37
00:02:14,828 --> 00:02:17,238
Those are real Airwallex sandbox IDs.

38
00:02:17,238 --> 00:02:21,949
The transfer went from scheduled to paid, using the sandbox simulator.

39
00:02:29,596 --> 00:02:33,185
Anyone can verify an exported copy, offline. It passes.

40
00:02:33,185 --> 00:02:37,315
Edit one number in the copy, and it fails, and names the row.

41
00:02:39,980 --> 00:02:42,438
Built on Airwallex's sandbox APIs:

42
00:02:42,438 --> 00:02:46,991
balances, rates, quotes, conversions, beneficiaries, transfers.

43
00:02:46,991 --> 00:02:48,798
The code is MIT licensed,

44
00:02:48,798 --> 00:02:52,201
and the repository is linked with this entry.

